gm-method/rendering (a known visual bug whose code-level cull math is provably correct yet the user still sees it, AND the headless harness structurally cannot reproduce the live gameplay condition): instead of chasing a phantom math fix, ship a ROBUST structural change that makes the bug class impossible. Concrete instance: 'objects of system A (native THREE.BatchedMesh per-instance cull, re-tested vs the live camera every frame) never vanish on close approach, but objects of system B (@three.ez InstancedMesh2 + a per-instance BVH frustum cull) do, whole-object, when nearest+looking-level, recovering on pitch-up.' Tracing the real lib source eliminates every code-level cause (union bounding box stamped before computeBVH so the per-instance box is correct; sort-based LOD-drop already off; addInstance DOES bvh.insert so no stale-BVH; bvh.js applies margin symmetrically grow-at-insert/shrink-at-test so no inversion; the n-vertex frustum-AABB test is correct even when the camera is inside the box). The lib onFrustumEnter hook is REJECT-ONLY (AND-gated after the BVH pass) so it cannot rescue a dropped instance. The robust fix: WRAP performFrustumCulling on each parent InstancedMesh2 -- call the original, then unconditionally append any active instance within a near radius (first-LOD distance + a few m) of the camera to LOD0's render list (levels[0].object.instanceIndex.array) and bump lod.count[0] + levels[0].object.count + set instanceIndex._needsUpdate. The nearest ring is a handful of objects always essentially on screen, so force-drawing them is cheap and gives system B the same always-near-drawn guarantee system A gets for free. Witness via a source-shape regression test locking the wrap on every sub-mesh + a window counter, since the harness cannot hold the gameplay session. Also: the gm browser verb re-navigates on every dispatch and only captures a fixed post-nav console/network window -- async probes that wait for late-streaming runtime state fire after capture stops, so a synchronous in-page console.log or a network-URL smuggle inside the capture window is the only reliable readout, and a close-approach gameplay bug is genuinely blocked-on-user-capture there.